---
title: "Code cells inside fenced divs"
---

A `:::` container folds its children into one block, so for as long as the executor only
scanned top-level blocks a code cell inside a container was dead source: it rendered, and
it never ran. This document is the shape that pins the fix (backlog item 210) — one
executable cell per container kind, so each one carries the output slot the executor
fills in place.

## A cell in a callout

::: {.callout-note}
The output belongs inside the callout, under the code that produced it, not after the
box closes.

```{python}
print("from inside a callout")
```
:::

## A cell in a layout grid

::: {layout-ncol=2}

```{python}
print("left column")
```

```{python}
print("right column")
```

:::

## A cell in a width escape

::: {.column-page}
A wide container is where a table too broad for the reading measure goes, which is
exactly the cell an author most wants to run.

```{python}
print("| a | b |")
print("|---|---|")
print("| 1 | 2 |")
```
:::

## A cell in a titled callout

::: {.callout-tip title="Sum of the first n integers"}
The sum $\sum_{i=1}^{n} i$ equals $n(n+1)/2$.

```{python}
print(sum(range(1, 5)) == 4 * 5 // 2)
```
:::

## A cell two containers deep

A container folds another container, and the cells keep their slots at the depth they
were written. This is also the shape that used to warn: nothing nested could run.

::: {.column-page}
::: {.callout-tip}
```{python}
print("two levels down")
```
:::
:::

## A `{js}` cell earns no slot

A `{js}` cell mounts its own live target in the browser and never produces a server-side
output block, so no slot is emitted for one — an element that could never fill.

::: {.callout-note}
```{js}
const ul = document.createElement("ul");
for (const n of [1, 2, 3]) {
  const li = document.createElement("li");
  li.textContent = `${n} doubled is ${n * 2}`;
  ul.appendChild(li);
}
return ul;
```
:::
